{% from "components/almanac.njk" import docsCta %}

{% macro matrixMark(mark) %}
  {% if mark.state == "yes" %}
    {% set markLabel = "Built in" %}
    {% set markSymbol = "✓" %}
  {% elif mark.state == "partial" %}
    {% set markLabel = "Partial or adjacent" %}
    {% set markSymbol = "◐" %}
  {% elif mark.state == "no" %}
    {% set markLabel = "Not in this scope" %}
    {% set markSymbol = "—" %}
  {% else %}
    {% set markLabel = "Not established by current sources" %}
    {% set markSymbol = "?" %}
  {% endif %}
  <span class="matrix-mark matrix-mark-{{ mark.state }}" title="{{ mark.note }}"><span aria-hidden="true">{{ markSymbol }}</span><span class="sr-only">{{ markLabel }}: {{ mark.note }}</span></span>
{% endmacro %}

<header class="architecture-hero">
  <div class="architecture-hero-gridlines" aria-hidden="true"></div>
  <div class="shell architecture-hero-copy">
    <p class="architecture-eyebrow"><span>{{ release.tag }}</span> System architecture</p>
    <h1>The gateway runs the model;<br><span>the broker authorizes effects.</span></h1>
    <p class="architecture-lede">The gateway handles chat and model inference. A separate broker authenticates the request, applies static Cedar policy and hard execution limits, then runs one Wasm provider operation.</p>
    <div class="architecture-actions">
      <a class="button button-primary" href="#runtime-map">See the runtime path <span aria-hidden="true">↓</span></a>
      <a class="button button-secondary" href="#comparison">Compare agent runtimes <span aria-hidden="true">↓</span></a>
    </div>
    <div class="architecture-proof" role="group" aria-label="Architecture summary">
      <div><strong>1</strong><span>proposal boundary</span></div>
      <div><strong>2</strong><span>daemon processes</span></div>
      <div><strong>3</strong><span>broker host interfaces</span></div>
      <div><strong>0</strong><span>models in authorization</span></div>
    </div>
    <p class="architecture-release-note"><strong>Pre-production:</strong> the Dekopon diagram and row are pinned to the published {{ release.tag }} release. Competitor sources were checked <time datetime="{{ architecture.checkedDateIso }}">{{ architecture.checkedDate }}</time>.</p>
  </div>
</header>

<section class="architecture-map-section" id="runtime-map">
  <div class="shell">
    <div class="architecture-section-heading">
      <p class="section-kicker">Process state · Tokio tasks · provider calls</p>
      <h2>The gateway shares process state and spawns one session task per message.</h2>
      <p>The diagram separates daemon-lifetime state from per-message and per-call state. Cedar decides over startup-fixed policy, constraint sets bind the allowed reach, and Wasmtime creates a fresh store and component instance for each authorized provider call.</p>
    </div>

    <figure class="architecture-system-map" aria-labelledby="architecture-map-title">
      <figcaption id="architecture-map-title" class="sr-only">Dekopon runtime and provider supply-chain architecture</figcaption>
      <div class="architecture-runtime-map">
        <div class="map-topline">
          <span>runtime / authority path</span>
          <code>request ↓ &nbsp; result ↑</code>
        </div>

        <div class="map-surfaces map-integrations">
          <span>gateway integrations · one reader task each</span>
          <div>
            <strong>Slack</strong><strong>Discord</strong><strong>Telegram</strong><strong>WhatsApp</strong><strong>local socket</strong>
          </div>
          <p>Service integrations authenticate their connection or envelope and derive a canonical subject plus chat coordinates; the owner-only local development socket instead trusts its caller to declare a <code>dev.*</code> subject. <code>dekopon console</code> joins at the session loop without a transport reader or router.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="map-arrow" aria-hidden="true"><i></i><span>bounded mpsc · transport-derived event</span></div>

        <div class="map-process map-gateway-process">
          <div class="map-process-heading">
            <span>gateway · injects integration-derived subject, never principal</span>
            <strong><code>dekopond</code></strong>
          </div>
          <div class="map-shared-state">
            <small>shared process state · one per daemon</small>
            <div>
              <span>routing table</span><span>bounded inbound queue</span><span>session semaphore</span><span>conversation + asset stores</span><span>model client cache + connection pools</span>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="map-flow-stack map-gateway-flow">
            <article class="map-node map-flow-node">
              <small>01 · routing loop</small>
              <strong>one event → one routed message</strong>
              <span>First-match routing and address checks run on the central loop; its <code>JoinSet</code> spawns one async session task for each accepted chat message.</span>
            </article>
            <article class="map-node map-flow-node map-node-agent">
              <small>02 · async session task</small>
              <strong>admit, attest, then seed</strong>
              <span>A process semaphore bounds concurrency; an in-flight set serializes the same transport/channel/thread. A fresh <code>capabilitiesForChat</code> broker call precedes model spend, then optional history and assets are loaded.</span>
            </article>
            <article class="map-node map-flow-node map-node-model">
              <small>03 · blocking prompt loop</small>
              <strong>shared model client ↔ external API</strong>
              <span><code>spawn_blocking</code> keeps synchronous model and shell work off Tokio workers. Each model turn sends instructions, optional history, the message, and tool definitions through one lazily cached client and connection pool. The route default is eight model turns; one turn may return at most ten tool calls.</span>
            </article>
            <article class="map-node map-flow-node map-node-shell">
              <small>04 · each <code>bash</code> call</small>
              <strong>one fresh script interpreter</strong>
              <span>Scripts may use loops, conditionals, functions, builtins, and provider command words. The interpreter starts no OS processes; capability words become broker proposals. Work is bounded: the defaults cap one script at 100,000 steps and 30 seconds, while one session shares a 16-capability-call budget across every script.</span>
            </article>
          </div>
          <div class="map-loop-note"><span>bounded repeat</span><strong>model request → <code>bash</code> script → tool results → next model request</strong><small>until a final answer, cancellation, refusal, or budget exhaustion</small></div>
        </div>

        <div class="map-boundary" role="separator" aria-label="Untrusted proposal boundary">
          <span>untrusted <code>InvocationRequest</code> proposal + <code>ChatAttestation</code></span>
          <strong>one bounded Unix request per connection</strong>
          <span><code>InvocationResult</code> · never authority</span>
        </div>

        <div class="map-process map-broker-process">
          <div class="map-process-heading">
            <span>tool execution · authorization + provider host</span>
            <strong><code>dekopon-brokerd</code></strong>
          </div>
          <div class="map-shared-state map-broker-state">
            <small>startup-fixed process state · shared by every connection task</small>
            <div>
              <span>UID → peer + attestor map</span><span>Cedar <code>PolicyEngine</code> + digest</span><span>constraint + credential catalogs</span><span>replay ledger + audit writer</span><span>one Wasmtime <code>Engine</code> + <code>Linker</code></span><span>one compiled <code>InstancePre</code> per provider</span>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="map-flow-stack map-broker-flow">
            <article class="map-node map-flow-node map-node-connection">
              <small>01 · Tokio connection task</small>
              <strong>authenticate peer + decode frame</strong>
              <span>The listener admits under a connection semaphore, spawns one task, reads OS peer credentials, maps the UID, and strict-decodes one size- and deadline-bounded request.</span>
            </article>
            <article class="map-node map-flow-node map-node-identity">
              <small>02 · identity envelope</small>
              <strong>validate attestation → derive context</strong>
              <span>The broker binds proposal ID to attestation, checks chat scope and the gateway’s attestor grant, then maps the integration-derived subject to a principal, Agent actor, <code>via</code>, and chat context. The payload cannot name its principal.</span>
            </article>
            <article class="map-node map-flow-node map-node-cedar">
              <small>03 · Cedar request</small>
              <strong>trusted context + capability → policy input</strong>
              <span>After replay reservation and constraint lookup, the broker constructs principal × action × provider × trusted routing context. Message text and provider JSON input do not enter Cedar.</span>
            </article>
            <article class="map-node map-flow-node map-node-limits">
              <small>04 · startup-fixed policy</small>
              <strong>evaluate → deny or bind constraints</strong>
              <span>Policies parse and strict-validate once before the listener binds. Per proposal, Cedar returns allow or deny; an allow is bound to owner-authored provider, host, method, call, byte, and deadline constraints as one broker-only <code>AuthorizedInvocation</code>. Symbolic credential selection stays alongside it, never inside it.</span>
            </article>
            <article class="map-node map-flow-node map-node-runtime">
              <small>05 · provider dispatch</small>
              <strong>route authorization to compiled provider</strong>
              <span>The decision is audited before execution. The broker selects the capability route and optional destination-bound credential, then passes the single-use authorization to the Wasmtime host.</span>
            </article>
          </div>
          <div class="map-broker-services">
            <div><span>replay protection</span><span>credential selection</span><span>decision + terminal audit</span></div>
            <strong>one authorization · one provider invocation</strong>
          </div>
          <div class="map-provider-host">
            <div class="map-provider-host-label"><span>Wasmtime lifecycle</span><code>compile once · instantiate per authorized call</code></div>
            <div class="map-provider-lifecycle">
              <div><small>broker startup</small><strong><code>provider.wasm</code> → Cranelift → <code>InstancePre</code></strong><span><code>Component::new</code> compiles; <code>instantiate_pre</code> resolves imports once. An optional content-addressed cache can skip recompilation after restart.</span></div>
              <i aria-hidden="true">→</i>
              <div><small>each allowed proposal</small><strong>fresh <code>Store</code> + component instance</strong><span>Fuel, memory, host calls, bytes, and wall time start fresh; <code>instantiate_async</code> reuses the compiled pre-instantiation.</span></div>
            </div>
            <div class="map-provider-component">
              <small>host world · <code>dekopon:broker-provider/provider@0.2.0</code></small>
              <strong><code>invoke(capability, input-json)</code></strong>
              <span>Calls the <code>dekopon:provider/provider@0.2.0</code> export. Only declared Dekopon imports are linked; generic WASI is absent.</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="map-wit-seam"><span>only declared WIT imports, under this invocation’s exact grant</span></div>
        <div class="map-host-services">
          <article><small><code>dekopon:http/client@1.0.0</code></small><strong>native HTTP</strong><span>DNS pinning · no redirects · late credential injection</span></article>
          <article><small><code>dekopon:storage/jsonl@0.1.0</code></small><strong>JSONL</strong><span>opaque namespace · quota · transaction</span></article>
          <article><small><code>dekopon:storage/durable-files@0.1.0</code></small><strong>durable files</strong><span>logical names · quota · transaction</span></article>
          <article><small>no import</small><strong>pure compute</strong><span>Python · ripgrep · transforms</span></article>
        </div>
        <div class="map-world-row">
          <span>external APIs</span>
          <i aria-hidden="true"></i>
          <span>broker-owned volume</span>
        </div>
      </div>

      <aside class="architecture-build-rail" aria-label="Provider build and distribution path">
        <div class="build-rail-top"><span>build / ship</span><code>offline from requests</code></div>
        <ol>
          <li><span>01</span><div><strong>Provider source</strong><small>Rust SDK + manifest + tests</small></div></li>
          <li><span>02</span><div><strong>WIT packages</strong><small><code>wkg</code> fetches versioned interfaces</small></div></li>
          <li><span>03</span><div><strong><code>wasm32-unknown-unknown</code></strong><small>dependencies close at build time</small></div></li>
          <li><span>04</span><div><strong><code>wasm-tools component</code></strong><small>componentize + inspect imports</small></div></li>
          <li><span>05</span><div><strong>Test the real boundary</strong><small>direct host or provider SDK testkit</small></div></li>
          <li class="build-rail-artifact"><span>06</span><div><strong><code>provider.wasm</code></strong><small>checksum · provenance · OCI artifact</small></div></li>
        </ol>
        <div class="build-rail-install"><span aria-hidden="true">←</span><strong>operator stages bytes before broker startup</strong></div>
        <p>The broker loads local component files. It doesn’t pull OCI artifacts, verify attestations, or hot-reload providers yet.</p>
      </aside>
    </figure>

    <div class="architecture-role-grid">
      <article class="reveal"><span>gateway lifetime</span><h3>Gateway state outlives messages.</h3><p>Transport readers, routes, admission, history, assets, and lazily built model connection pools are shared process state rather than rebuilt for each chat message.</p></article>
      <article class="reveal reveal-delay-1"><span>message lifetime</span><h3>A chat thread is state, not a task.</h3><p>Every routed message gets a new async session task and a blocking prompt worker. The in-flight registry serializes one conversation; persistent history remains bounded data in the gateway.</p></article>
      <article class="reveal reveal-delay-2"><span>broker lifetime</span><h3>Each broker operation gets one connection task.</h3><p>Every capability proposal uses a fresh one-request Unix connection. All connection tasks share the broker’s fixed identity, policy, constraints, credentials, audit, and compiled provider registry.</p></article>
      <article class="reveal reveal-delay-3"><span>call lifetime</span><h3>Provider code compiles once and instantiates per call.</h3><p>Compilation or cache loading and import resolution happen at broker startup. An allowed proposal gets a fresh Wasmtime store and component instance before the exported <code>invoke</code> function runs.</p></article>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="runtime-comparison-section" id="comparison">
  <div class="shell">
    <div class="architecture-section-heading comparison-heading">
      <p class="section-kicker">{{ architecture.runtimes | length }} systems · {{ architecture.criteria | length }} architectural properties</p>
      <h2>Agent runtimes make different trust trades.</h2>
      <p>This is not a feature score. The columns ask where code runs, who holds authority, and what evidence survives. A partial mark means the property is optional, narrower than the header, or supplied by an adjacent service.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="matrix-legend" role="group" aria-label="Comparison mark legend">
      <span><i class="matrix-mark matrix-mark-yes" aria-hidden="true">✓</i> built in</span>
      <span><i class="matrix-mark matrix-mark-partial" aria-hidden="true">◐</i> partial or adjacent</span>
      <span><i class="matrix-mark matrix-mark-no" aria-hidden="true">—</i> not in this scope</span>
      <span><i class="matrix-mark matrix-mark-unknown" aria-hidden="true">?</i> current sources don’t establish it</span>
    </div>
    <p class="matrix-scope-warning"><strong>AWS renamed Bedrock Agents to Bedrock Agents Classic.</strong> It entered maintenance mode and closed to new customers on 30 July 2026. AgentCore now has its own row; the Classic row doesn’t inherit AgentCore capabilities.</p>
    <p class="matrix-tradeoff-note"><strong>Dekopon is marked “no” for tool reuse.</strong> It is not a general-purpose isolation environment. Existing CLIs, SDK tools, MCP servers, and plugins need a purpose-built Wasm provider or adapter before the broker can host them.</p>

    <div class="runtime-matrix-wrap" tabindex="0" aria-label="Scrollable agent runtime comparison">
      <table class="runtime-matrix">
        <caption class="sr-only">{% for runtime in architecture.runtimes %}{{ runtime.name }}{% if not loop.last %}, {% endif %}{% endfor %} compared across {{ architecture.criteria | length }} architecture properties</caption>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col" class="runtime-column">Agent system</th>
            {% for criterion in architecture.criteria %}
              <th scope="col"><span>{{ criterion.short }}</span><small>{{ criterion.label }}</small></th>
            {% endfor %}
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {% for runtime in architecture.runtimes %}
            <tr{% if runtime.featured %} class="runtime-featured"{% endif %}>
              <th scope="row" class="runtime-column">
                <a href="#sources-{{ runtime.key }}">{{ runtime.name }}</a>
                <small>{{ runtime.scope }}</small>
              </th>
              {% for criterion in architecture.criteria %}
                {% set mark = runtime.marks[criterion.key] %}
                <td>{{ matrixMark(mark) }}</td>
              {% endfor %}
            </tr>
          {% endfor %}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="matrix-scroll-note">The first column stays fixed while the properties scroll. Hover a mark for its note; every reason is also written below.</p>

    <details class="criteria-disclosure">
      <summary>Read the exact definition behind every column</summary>
      <div class="criteria-grid">
        {% for criterion in architecture.criteria %}
          <article><span>{% if loop.index < 10 %}0{% endif %}{{ loop.index }}</span><h3>{{ criterion.label }}</h3><p>{{ criterion.description }}</p></article>
        {% endfor %}
      </div>
    </details>

    <div class="score-notes">
      <h3>Every mark has a written reason.</h3>
      <p>The table stays scannable; these disclosures preserve the qualification behind each symbol.</p>
      {% for runtime in architecture.runtimes %}
        <details>
          <summary><strong>{{ runtime.name }}</strong><span>{{ runtime.scope }}</span></summary>
          <dl>
            {% for criterion in architecture.criteria %}
              {% set mark = runtime.marks[criterion.key] %}
              <div><dt>{{ criterion.label }}</dt><dd><span class="score-state score-state-{{ mark.state }}">{{ mark.state }}</span>{{ mark.note }}</dd></div>
            {% endfor %}
          </dl>
        </details>
      {% endfor %}
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="architecture-limits-section" id="limits">
  <div class="shell">
    <div class="architecture-section-heading architecture-limits-heading">
      <p class="section-kicker">Current limits in {{ release.tag }}</p>
      <h2>Dekopon’s checked row doesn’t make it production-ready.</h2>
      <p>The marks describe implemented boundaries. They don’t erase the deployment and product gaps below.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="architecture-limit-grid">
      <article><span>UID</span><h3>One Unix UID is one trust domain.</h3><p>The gateway and broker are separate processes, but a hostile process already running as that UID can act as the configured peer. A dedicated gateway UID is still future work.</p></article>
      <article><span>audit</span><h3>The audit anchor is local.</h3><p>The chain detects mutation and prefix rollback relative to its checkpoint. Anyone who can roll back both local files can defeat that comparison.</p></article>
      <article><span>memory</span><h3>Durable recall stays on demand.</h3><p>Dekopon has no automatic durable replay, semantic or vector memory, cross-agent sharing, deletion/export UI, or content-encryption claim.</p></article>
      <article><span>providers</span><h3>Operators stage provider bytes.</h3><p>The broker loads local files at startup. It doesn’t pull from OCI, pin an expected digest, verify artifact provenance, or reload a replacement live.</p></article>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="architecture-sources-section" id="sources">
  <div class="shell architecture-sources-layout">
